---
import Project from '../layouts/Project.astro';
import Section from '../components/Section.astro';
import Panel from '../components/Panel.astro';
import { project } from '../data/projects';
const p = project('git.agentic');
const repo = p.repo;
const faqs = [
  {
    question: 'What is git.agentic?',
    answer: 'git.agentic is open-source version control for AI agent behavior. It snapshots code, prompts, tools, model configuration, memory, and schema as one coherent version.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Why is Git alone not enough to roll back an AI agent?',
    answer: 'Git restores code, but an agent can remain broken because its prompts, model, tool interfaces, database memory, or schema moved independently. A git.agentic commit versions all six dimensions. Rollback restores prompts, tracked memory and schema directly, and records the code, tools and model that the operator must redeploy.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Which AI agent frameworks does git.agentic support?',
    answer: 'The public Python SDK includes a drop-in LangGraph checkpointer. The architecture also defines a framework-neutral sidecar integration for the Claude Agent SDK.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Is git.agentic used in production?',
    answer: 'Not yet. The open-source MVP is complete and production validation is in progress. Design-partner use in real deployments is the bar for v1.0.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Is git.agentic open source?',
    answer: 'Yes. The Rust core, CLI, daemon, Python SDK, and runnable broken-prompt demo are available on GitHub under the Apache 2.0 license.',
  },
];
const docs = [
  { label: 'README', href: `${repo}#readme` },
  { label: 'MVP spec', href: `${repo}/blob/main/docs/product/mvp-spec.md` },
  { label: 'Snapshot model', href: `${repo}/blob/main/docs/architecture/snapshot-model.md` },
  { label: 'ADRs', href: `${repo}/tree/main/docs/adr` },
  { label: 'Threat model', href: `${repo}/blob/main/git.agentic-threat-model.md` },
  { label: 'Security policy', href: `${repo}/blob/main/SECURITY.md` },
  { label: 'Contributing', href: `${repo}/blob/main/CONTRIBUTING.md` },
  { label: 'License', href: `${repo}/blob/main/LICENSE` },
];
---
<Project
  project={p}
  title="AI Agent Version Control & Atomic Rollback | git.agentic"
  description="Version AI agent code, prompts, tools, model, memory and schema as one commit. Rollback restores prompts, memory and schema and records what to redeploy."
  heading="Version control and atomic rollback for AI agent behavior."
  softwareDescription="Open-source version control and atomic rollback for AI agent behavior."
  programmingLanguage={['Rust', 'Python']}
  faqs={faqs}
  docs={docs}
>
  <Fragment slot="lead">
    Six things determine how an agent acts: code, prompts, tools, model,
    memory and schema. A git.agentic commit versions all six. Rollback
    restores prompts, tracked memory and schema directly, and records the
    code, tools and model that the operator must redeploy.
  </Fragment>

  <Fragment slot="status">
    <div><dt>Maturity</dt><dd>The MVP code is complete and has been public on <code>main</code> since 22 May 2026.</dd></div>
    <div><dt>Phase</dt><dd>Production validation. Design-partner use in real deployments is the bar for v1.0.</dd></div>
    <div><dt>Production use</dt><dd>None yet. Production validation is in progress.</dd></div>
    <div><dt>Not yet done</dt><dd>Rollback under 5 seconds at 1M-row pgvector scale is under verification (<a href={`${repo}/issues/132`}>issue #132</a>). Per-operation authorization beyond rollback is open (TM-001).</dd></div>
    <div><dt>Language</dt><dd>Rust core, CLI and daemon. Python SDK.</dd></div>
    <div><dt>License</dt><dd>Apache 2.0</dd></div>
  </Fragment>

  <Section id="quickstart" title="Quickstart" note="Needs Podman or Docker with compose, cargo, Python 3.10 or later, and psql.">
    <p>
      One clone and one script. <code>run-demo.sh</code> starts Postgres and
      pgvector with Podman or Docker compose, builds the daemon and the CLI,
      runs the agent, deploys the bad change, rolls it back and tears the
      database down again. CI replays the same demo end to end on every pull
      request.
    </p>
    <Panel title="shell" copy>
<pre><code>$ git clone https://github.com/git-agentic/git.agentic
$ cd git.agentic/examples/langgraph-rollback
$ ./scripts/run-demo.sh             <span class="comment"># starts Postgres, builds, runs, breaks, rolls back</span></code></pre>
    </Panel>
  </Section>

  <Section id="features" title="Features" note="What the open source MVP contains.">
    <div class="grid-2">
      <div class="card feature">
        <h3>One version for six dimensions</h3>
        <p>A commit captures code, prompts, tools, model, memory and schema atomically.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card feature">
        <h3>Rollback</h3>
        <p><code>agentic rollback</code> restores prompts, tracked memory and schema, including reverse schema migrations. It records the target's code, tools and model, which the operator redeploys.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card feature">
        <h3>Six-dimension diff</h3>
        <p><code>agentic diff</code> shows what changed in each dimension between two versions.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card feature">
        <h3>Memory snapshots</h3>
        <p>The state of the tracked Postgres and pgvector tables is captured at commit time and restored on rollback. It is the one memory backend in the MVP.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card feature">
        <h3>MCP tool fingerprints</h3>
        <p>The tools dimension records MCP server fingerprints, so a version knows which tools the agent saw. Rollback records them and does not apply them.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card feature">
        <h3>Python SDK and LangGraph</h3>
        <p>A typed Python client with a drop-in LangGraph checkpointer. The architecture also defines a sidecar integration for the Claude Agent SDK.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card feature">
        <h3><code>agentic</code> CLI</h3>
        <p>Core commands: <code>init</code>, <code>commit</code>, <code>log</code>, <code>diff</code>, <code>rollback</code> and <code>status</code>, plus <code>ping</code>, <code>resolve</code> and <code>cat-object</code>.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card feature">
        <h3><code>agenticd</code> daemon</h3>
        <p>A Rust daemon that owns the object store and the snapshot engine.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <p class="note">
      Not in the MVP: web UI, hosted SaaS, eval and CI pipelines, MCP registry,
      sandbox execution, A2A routing, additional memory backends and
      additional frameworks.
      <a href={`${repo}/blob/main/docs/adr/0001-architecture-foundations.md`}>ADR-0001</a>
      sections 9 and 10 explain why.
    </p>
  </Section>

  <Section id="how-it-works" title="How it works">
    <h3>The version</h3>
    <p>
      <code>git revert</code> knows about code. It does not know about prompts,
      memory state, schema versions or model versions, so it cannot put a
      regressed agent back together. A git.agentic commit records the whole
      tuple.
    </p>
    <Panel title="AgentVersion">
<pre><code><span class="comment"># a commit captures this atomically</span>
AgentVersion = (
    code_sha,
    prompts,
    tools,
    model,
    memory_snapshot,
    schema_version,
)</code></pre>
    </Panel>
    <div class="table-wrap" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="The six dimensions and what rollback does with each">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th scope="col">Dimension</th><th scope="col">What it is</th><th scope="col">On rollback</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td><code>code_sha</code></td><td>The git commit. The part you already version.</td><td>Recorded, not checked out. The operator redeploys that commit.</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>prompts</code></td><td>System prompts, role prompts and few-shot templates, content-hashed.</td><td>Restored. The prompts directory is rewritten from the target.</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>tools</code></td><td>MCP server fingerprints. The tools the agent saw.</td><td>Recorded, not applied.</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>model</code></td><td>Provider, model id and decoding parameters. Pinned, not floated.</td><td>Recorded. The operator redeploys if it changed.</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>memory_snapshot</code></td><td>State of the tracked Postgres and pgvector tables at commit time.</td><td>Restored.</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>schema_version</code></td><td>The memory schema, with forward and reverse migrations.</td><td>Restored by running the reverse migrations.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h3>The broken-prompt demo</h3>
    <p>
      A prompt change ships together with a schema migration and contaminated
      memory rows, and breaks the agent. Restoring the prompt file, which is
      what <code>git revert</code> achieves, does not fix it: the schema is
      still migrated and the contaminated rows are still in Postgres.
      <code>agentic rollback</code> runs the reverse migration, restores the
      tracked memory and rewrites the prompts. The scenario is written up in
      <a href={`${repo}/blob/main/docs/product/demo-scenario.md`}>the demo scenario</a>.
    </p>
    <p>
      The excerpt below is from the recorded run in
      <a href={`${repo}/blob/main/examples/langgraph-rollback/demo.cast`}><code>demo.cast</code></a>.
      Setup, the build, a few progress lines and the final log are left out.
    </p>
    <Panel title="./scripts/run-demo.sh, excerpt from demo.cast">
<pre><code><span class="dim">=== 4. baseline ask ===</span>
<span class="ok">&gt; I understand. Could you tell me a bit more about why? I'd like to help find the right outcome for you.

Looking at your account history, I see: Customer thanked support for the quick reply.</span>

<span class="dim">=== 5. commit baseline ===</span>
[20eb9cf] committed to langgraph/abbbd1ad6698ea81 as 20eb9cf892835ed7c37574075a51d2a04482ffe36596a9f6ff16c793af3a7bea
baseline = 20eb9cf892835ed7c37574075a51d2a04482ffe36596a9f6ff16c793af3a7bea

<span class="dim">=== 6. deploy bad change ===</span>
→ swapping prompts/system.txt for the sycophantic variant
→ applying migration 001_add_sentiment (episodes.sentiment NOT NULL)
→ INSERTing 5 contaminated rows into episodes
✓ bad change deployed

<span class="dim">=== 7. bad ask  (hallucinated refund + contaminated memory) ===</span>
