  - restore memory from manifest d9f76eb
  - rewrite ~/git.agentic/examples/langgraph-rollback/prompts from target prompts tree 2e6a8d6
  - model version captured in target — operator must redeploy if it changed
  - forward-recorded rollback as 2a2893a on langgraph/abbbd1ad6698ea81
✓ rollback complete; HEAD now at 2a2893a</span>
live schema / sentiment columns after rollback: 0.0.0/0

<span class="dim">=== 11. ask again — empathetic answer + clean memory ===</span>
<span class="ok">&gt; I understand. Could you tell me a bit more about why? I'd like to help find the right outcome for you.

Looking at your account history, I see: Customer thanked support for the quick reply.</span></code></pre>
    </Panel>
  </Section>

  <Section id="security" title="Security and limits" note="What the daemon enforces today, and what is still open.">
    <dl class="rows">
      <div>
        <dt>Socket peer authentication</dt>
        <dd>The daemon checks socket peers against a UID allowlist (<a href={`${repo}/blob/main/docs/adr/0012-socket-peer-authentication.md`}>ADR-0012</a>).</dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>Approval for reverse migrations</dt>
        <dd>
          Any rollback that runs reverse schema migrations needs an operator
          approval token (<a href={`${repo}/blob/main/docs/adr/0019-approval-for-reverse-migration-rollback.md`}>ADR-0019</a>).
          Tokens are single-use per daemon process and scoped, so a
          reverse-migration token cannot authorize a data-loss rollback. The
          <code>agentic</code> CLI can attach the token automatically when its
          preview shows approval is needed, it can read the key, and the
          daemon's peer UID owns that key.
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>Sidecar isolation</dt>
        <dd>
          The sidecar deployment keeps daemon state on a volume only the
          daemon writes, checks state-directory ownership at startup, reads
          credentials from files, and redacts the GCS token and the Postgres
          URL in the store and memory-backend debug output. The Cloud Run
          layout is not yet verified, and full credential isolation needs
          separate service identities.
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>Threat model</dt>
        <dd>
          The <a href={`${repo}/blob/main/git.agentic-threat-model.md`}>threat model</a>
          is reconciled (TM-001 to TM-013) and documents the remaining risks.
        </dd>
      </div>
    </dl>
    <div class="callout">
      <h3>Still open</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>
          The rollback target of under 5 seconds at 1M-row pgvector scale is
          under verification (<a href={`${repo}/issues/132`}>issue #132</a>).
          Demo-scale rollback takes about 1 second.
        </li>
        <li>
          Per-operation authorization is still open (TM-001). For example, a
          rollback without a schema change can restore memory without an
          approval token.
        </li>
        <li>git.agentic is not in production use anywhere yet.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </Section>

  <Section id="changes" title="Recent changes" note="November 2026">
    <div class="changes">
      <h3>20 November 2026: security hardening round</h3>
      <p>Merged as PRs #145 to #153 and #156.</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Rollbacks that run reverse schema migrations now need an operator approval token (<a href={`${repo}/blob/main/docs/adr/0019-approval-for-reverse-migration-rollback.md`}>ADR-0019</a>).</li>
        <li>The object store keeps raw and typed objects at separate storage paths, so one can no longer occupy the other's address (<a href={`${repo}/blob/main/docs/adr/0018-separate-raw-object-namespace.md`}>ADR-0018</a>).</li>
        <li>The sidecar deployment keeps daemon state on a volume only the daemon writes and reads credentials from files. Full credential isolation still needs separate service identities, as the security section above says.</li>
        <li>Rollback validates the prompt tree before any database change and refuses symlinked prompt directories.</li>
        <li>The LangGraph checkpointer only deserializes allow-listed types.</li>
        <li>CI actions are pinned to commit SHAs and the sidecar's base images to digests. The open Rust advisories were fixed.</li>
        <li>The threat model was reconciled, TM-001 to TM-013.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </Section>

  <Section id="partners" title="Design partners">
    <p>
      If you run a stateful LangGraph agent in production and a prompt or
      schema change has left you with a regression you could not cleanly
      revert, get in touch at
      <a href="mailto:toni@git-agentic.com">toni@git-agentic.com</a>.
    </p>
  </Section>
</Project>
